Your project targets **.NET 10**, so the best approach for `File.*` and `Directory.*` is the **`System.IO.Abstractions`** NuGet package — it's battle-tested and already provides `IFileSystem` with mock support.

## Setup

**1. Install packages:**
```bash
dotnet add package System.IO.Abstractions
dotnet add package System.IO.Abstractions.TestingHelpers  # test project only
```

**2. Register in DI (Program.cs):**
```csharp
builder.Services.AddSingleton<IFileSystem, FileSystem>();
```

**3. Inject `IFileSystem` into your classes:**
```csharp
using System.IO.Abstractions;

public class ConfigManager(IFileSystem fileSystem)
{
    public string Read(string path) => fileSystem.File.ReadAllText(path);

    public void Write(string path, string content) => fileSystem.File.WriteAllText(path, content);

    public bool DirectoryExists(string path) => fileSystem.Directory.Exists(path);
}
```

**4. Test with `MockFileSystem`:**
```csharp
using System.IO.Abstractions.TestingHelpers;

var mockFs = new MockFileSystem(new Dictionary<string, MockFileData>
{
    { "/data/config.json", new MockFileData("{\"key\": \"value\"}") }
});
mockFs.AddDirectory("/data");

var manager = new ConfigManager(mockFs);
Assert.True(manager.DirectoryExists("/data"));
Assert.Equal("{\"key\": \"value\"}", manager.Read("/data/config.json"));
```

---

**Why this over a custom wrapper?** `System.IO.Abstractions` covers the full `System.IO` surface (File, Directory, Path, etc.) so you won't need to extend it as usage grows. The `MockFileSystem` is an in-memory filesystem that handles path normalization, missing-file exceptions, etc. — far less work than maintaining your own mocks.

